1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,800
Tuesday, November fourteenth,
twenty seventeen.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:08,110
Nine forty-four in the morning, at
Minami-Nagareyama station,

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:09,650
just outside Tokyo.

4
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:11,730
A commuter train pulls out.

5
00:00:12,145 --> 00:00:13,335
Twenty seconds early.

6
00:00:13,837 --> 00:00:16,187
Nobody misses it. Nobody complains.

7
00:00:16,757 --> 00:00:19,027
The next train is four minutes behind.

8
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:22,467
But the company posts an
official apology anyway:

9
00:00:23,157 --> 00:00:27,967
"We deeply apologize for the severe
inconvenience imposed upon our customers."

10
00:00:28,576 --> 00:00:33,166
That story went around the world as proof
that Japanese trains are perfect.

11
00:00:33,676 --> 00:00:36,126
But here's what the
government's own data says.

12
00:00:36,696 --> 00:00:39,866
On Tokyo's most famous line, the Yamanote,

13
00:00:40,296 --> 00:00:46,206
the railway hands out official late notes on
about fifteen of every twenty weekdays,

14
00:00:46,416 --> 00:00:48,566
mostly for delays of a few minutes.

15
00:00:49,197 --> 00:00:51,707
So it's not that the trains
are never late.

16
00:00:52,077 --> 00:00:54,007
And it's not that the city is empty.

17
00:00:54,537 --> 00:00:58,707
At rush hour, some lines are still
sixty percent over capacity.

18
00:00:59,445 --> 00:01:04,275
Every day, Greater Tokyo's railways handle
around forty million trips.

19
00:01:04,685 --> 00:01:07,445
That's like ten New York City
subway systems,

20
00:01:07,485 --> 00:01:08,675
running at once.

21
00:01:09,273 --> 00:01:14,773
So how does a metro area of almost
thirty-seven million people move like this,

22
00:01:14,813 --> 00:01:16,543
without falling into chaos?

23
00:01:17,213 --> 00:01:19,603
The answer isn't one perfect machine.

24
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:23,323
It's a pile of strange, very human tricks.

25
00:01:23,941 --> 00:01:26,411
We'll start in the busiest
station on Earth,

26
00:01:26,721 --> 00:01:29,371
then squeeze into a
two-hundred-percent train,

27
00:01:29,781 --> 00:01:34,641
meet the railway that sells houses, and end
with the day the ground shook under

28
00:01:34,681 --> 00:01:36,151
nineteen bullet trains.

29
00:01:40,163 --> 00:01:41,493
Start at Shinjuku.

30
00:01:42,063 --> 00:01:46,153
Guinness World Records lists it as the
busiest railway station in the world.

31
00:01:46,706 --> 00:01:52,336
In twenty twenty-two, about two point seven
million people a day passed through it.

32
00:01:52,546 --> 00:01:55,516
That's four and a half times New
York's Penn Station.

33
00:01:56,006 --> 00:01:59,816
Every day, a whole Chicago walks
through one station.

34
00:02:00,246 --> 00:02:03,436
Before the pandemic, it was over
three and a half million.

35
00:02:04,041 --> 00:02:05,531
It's not one station.

36
00:02:05,901 --> 00:02:08,971
It's several, run by five
different companies,

37
00:02:09,381 --> 00:02:11,251
all stitched together underground.

38
00:02:11,841 --> 00:02:14,251
It's famous for getting even locals lost.

39
00:02:14,869 --> 00:02:18,959
And it was here, in nineteen
fifty-five, the story goes,

40
00:02:19,249 --> 00:02:22,719
that Tokyo invented one of its
most famous jobs.

41
00:02:23,189 --> 00:02:27,069
Station staff whose task was to push
passengers into the trains,

42
00:02:27,109 --> 00:02:28,919
so the doors could close.

43
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:32,530
The Japanese call them
oshiya, the pushers.

44
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:35,310
Many were students working part-time.

45
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:40,640
At their peak in the nineteen seventies,
Tokyo's rush-hour trains averaged two

46
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,890
hundred and twenty percent of capacity.

47
00:02:43,523 --> 00:02:46,693
Some still do this job today,
on a few lines,

48
00:02:46,863 --> 00:02:50,573
for about fifteen hundred yen an
hour, around ten dollars.

49
00:02:51,123 --> 00:02:55,333
One young woman, who goes by Haruka,
started in twenty twenty-one.

50
00:02:56,243 --> 00:02:59,173
She says it's mostly tucking
in coats and bags,

51
00:02:59,303 --> 00:03:00,573
so the doors can shut.

52
00:03:03,015 --> 00:03:06,995
Japan's transport ministry has an
official guide to crowding,

53
00:03:07,035 --> 00:03:08,785
and it's surprisingly specific.

54
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,390
At one hundred percent,
everyone has a seat,

55
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:14,130
a strap or a pole.

56
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:17,150
At one fifty, you can open a newspaper.

57
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:20,170
And at two hundred percent,
there's, quote,

58
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:24,950
"considerable body contact, but you can
just manage to read a magazine."

59
00:03:25,478 --> 00:03:30,488
For years, the most crowded stretch in
Japan was the Tozai subway line,

60
00:03:30,658 --> 00:03:33,028
between Kiba and Monzen-Nakacho.

61
00:03:33,598 --> 00:03:37,628
In twenty nineteen, it ran at a hundred
and ninety-nine percent.

62
00:03:38,110 --> 00:03:40,900
Today it's down to around a
hundred and sixty.

63
00:03:41,330 --> 00:03:47,190
But in one morning hour, sixty-four thousand
eight hundred people still squeeze

64
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:50,420
through that one stretch, on
twenty-seven trains.

65
00:03:50,930 --> 00:03:54,280
That's about twenty-four hundred
people in every train.

66
00:03:54,710 --> 00:03:56,980
A small town, every two minutes.

67
00:03:57,604 --> 00:04:02,174
Packing people that tightly created
its own problem. Groping.

68
00:04:02,724 --> 00:04:07,794
Tokyo first tried women-only cars back in
nineteen twelve, for schoolgirls.

69
00:04:08,224 --> 00:04:12,374
The modern version came in two thousand,
and by two thousand five,

70
00:04:12,784 --> 00:04:16,374
most Tokyo lines had women-only
cars at rush hour.

71
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:18,930
And here's the most surprising part.

72
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:23,550
Most small delays in Tokyo aren't
caused by the trains at all.

73
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:28,210
The ministry found that almost two out of
three are caused by passengers.

74
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:32,250
People holding doors, dropping
things, getting sick,

75
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:34,650
or just taking too long to get on.

76
00:04:35,129 --> 00:04:39,119
So when a train is late, the railway
gives you a slip of paper.

77
00:04:39,429 --> 00:04:42,859
A delay certificate, to show your
boss or your school.

78
00:04:43,349 --> 00:04:44,459
It's not a refund.

79
00:04:44,809 --> 00:04:46,139
It's an excuse note.

80
00:04:46,429 --> 00:04:50,439
And the government publishes how often
each line hands them out.

81
00:04:50,847 --> 00:04:55,837
The worst line, the Saikyo, issues them
on almost every weekday.

82
00:04:56,327 --> 00:05:00,157
The best, a small Tokyu line,
about once a month.

83
00:05:02,658 --> 00:05:04,528
So who runs all of this?

84
00:05:04,978 --> 00:05:06,948
Not one company. Dozens.

85
00:05:08,018 --> 00:05:11,648
JR East, Tokyo Metro, the
city's Toei subway,

86
00:05:11,938 --> 00:05:13,788
and a crowd of private railways.

87
00:05:14,438 --> 00:05:17,068
And many of those private
railways have a secret.

88
00:05:17,418 --> 00:05:19,088
They're also property companies.

89
00:05:19,690 --> 00:05:24,930
In nineteen twenty-three, a garden-city company
and its new railway opened a line

90
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:28,060
into the empty fields southwest of Tokyo.

91
00:05:28,590 --> 00:05:31,250
And next to the stations, they
started selling homes,

92
00:05:31,290 --> 00:05:34,780
in a planned suburb called Den-en-chofu.

93
00:05:35,233 --> 00:05:37,443
The plots went on sale in August.

94
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:41,983
Then, on September first, the Great
Kanto Earthquake hit,

95
00:05:42,373 --> 00:05:44,643
and much of central Tokyo burned.

96
00:05:45,053 --> 00:05:48,643
But the new suburb out in the
fields was barely damaged.

97
00:05:48,873 --> 00:05:50,883
And buyers rushed out of the city.

98
00:05:51,417 --> 00:05:54,347
The man running that railway
was Goto Keita.

99
00:05:54,877 --> 00:05:58,087
He built the company that's
known today as Tokyu.

100
00:05:58,577 --> 00:06:02,587
And he borrowed a trick from an Osaka
railway called Hankyu.

101
00:06:02,917 --> 00:06:06,487
Build the line, own the land around
it, sell the houses,

102
00:06:06,797 --> 00:06:09,077
and put a department store at
the end of the line,

103
00:06:09,117 --> 00:06:12,187
so the trains stay full in
both directions.

104
00:06:12,831 --> 00:06:14,001
It still works.

105
00:06:14,591 --> 00:06:19,711
In its latest financial year, Tokyu reportedly
made more profit from real estate

106
00:06:19,751 --> 00:06:21,401
than from running trains.

107
00:06:22,031 --> 00:06:24,661
The railway's best product
isn't the ticket.

108
00:06:24,971 --> 00:06:26,781
It's the home next to the station.

109
00:06:27,390 --> 00:06:30,360
And that's one reason Tokyo
grew the way it did.

110
00:06:30,910 --> 00:06:36,580
Not one downtown surrounded by highways, but busy
centers grown around the stations.

111
00:06:39,222 --> 00:06:41,852
Now, the most famous train in the world.

112
00:06:42,466 --> 00:06:47,976
On October first, nineteen sixty-four, nine
days before the Tokyo Olympics,

113
00:06:48,426 --> 00:06:52,576
the first Shinkansen line opened
between Tokyo and Osaka.

114
00:06:53,266 --> 00:06:56,756
Its trains ran at two hundred and
ten kilometers an hour.

115
00:06:57,126 --> 00:06:59,226
That's a hundred and thirty miles an hour,

116
00:06:59,266 --> 00:07:00,716
in nineteen sixty-four.

117
00:07:01,535 --> 00:07:04,505
But the two men who made it
happen had both left.

118
00:07:05,035 --> 00:07:08,655
The railway's president, Sogo Shinji,
and his chief engineer,

119
00:07:08,695 --> 00:07:15,015
Shima Hideo, resigned a year earlier, over
the project's huge cost overruns.

120
00:07:15,055 --> 00:07:18,585
By one account, neither was even
invited to the opening.

121
00:07:19,164 --> 00:07:23,684
Today, that line runs three hundred and
eighty-three trains a day,

122
00:07:23,724 --> 00:07:26,834
for four hundred and sixty
thousand passengers.

123
00:07:26,884 --> 00:07:32,714
And in twenty twenty-four, the average delay was
about one point four minutes per train.

124
00:07:33,064 --> 00:07:35,234
Including typhoons and earthquakes.

125
00:07:35,877 --> 00:07:37,167
Then there's the turnaround.

126
00:07:37,777 --> 00:07:44,107
At Tokyo Station, JR East's bullet trains heading north
often stop for only about twelve minutes.

127
00:07:44,817 --> 00:07:50,507
After the passengers get off, a cleaning crew has about
seven minutes to clean the entire train.

128
00:07:50,877 --> 00:07:52,227
One person per car.

129
00:07:52,477 --> 00:07:54,687
Every seat turned, every tray wiped.

130
00:07:55,225 --> 00:07:57,755
People call it the seven-minute miracle.

131
00:07:58,185 --> 00:08:00,095
And it wasn't always like this.

132
00:08:00,385 --> 00:08:05,565
In two thousand five, a safety veteran named
Teruo Yabe took over the cleaning

133
00:08:05,605 --> 00:08:09,035
company, when morale was low and
complaints were high.

134
00:08:09,526 --> 00:08:12,356
He started treating the seven
minutes like a performance,

135
00:08:12,546 --> 00:08:15,776
and let the cleaners suggest
their own improvements.

136
00:08:16,486 --> 00:08:19,356
Harvard Business School later wrote
a case study about it.

137
00:08:21,807 --> 00:08:26,407
Japan sits on top of some of the most
active fault lines in the world.

138
00:08:26,447 --> 00:08:29,117
So the trains have to listen
to the ground.

139
00:08:29,652 --> 00:08:35,432
In the late eighties, a seismologist named
Yutaka Nakamura designed a system that

140
00:08:35,472 --> 00:08:38,252
detects the first, weaker wave
of an earthquake,

141
00:08:38,292 --> 00:08:42,942
and cuts power to the trains before the
destructive shaking arrives.

142
00:08:43,392 --> 00:08:46,522
It went into use on the bullet train
in nineteen ninety-two.

143
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:54,330
On March eleventh, twenty eleven, a newer version of the
same idea faced the biggest test imaginable.

144
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:56,890
A magnitude nine earthquake.

145
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:00,020
A seismometer on the coast
caught the first wave,

146
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:02,470
and power was cut along the line.

147
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:06,570
Nineteen bullet trains were carrying
passengers in the region.

148
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:08,010
All of them stopped.

149
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:09,510
None of them derailed.

150
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,590
Nobody on board was hurt.

151
00:09:12,110 --> 00:09:16,760
In fact, bullet trains have derailed only
once with passengers on board,

152
00:09:17,130 --> 00:09:18,360
in two thousand four.

153
00:09:19,210 --> 00:09:20,680
Nobody was hurt then, either.

154
00:09:21,214 --> 00:09:23,744
But not every threat comes
from the ground.

155
00:09:24,294 --> 00:09:26,504
On March twentieth, nineteen ninety-five,

156
00:09:27,254 --> 00:09:33,354
members of the Aum Shinrikyo cult released sarin
nerve gas on five rush-hour subway

157
00:09:33,394 --> 00:09:35,124
trains in central Tokyo.

158
00:09:35,729 --> 00:09:41,869
At Kasumigaseki station, assistant
stationmaster Kazumasa Takahashi picked up a

159
00:09:41,909 --> 00:09:46,399
leaking bag with his hands and carried
it off the train. He died.

160
00:09:47,049 --> 00:09:52,239
In total, the attack killed fourteen people
and injured more than six thousand.

161
00:09:52,756 --> 00:09:57,606
That's why, for years, you'd struggle to find
a trash can in a Tokyo station.

162
00:09:58,316 --> 00:10:00,906
The bins were taken away after the attack.

163
00:10:01,316 --> 00:10:03,366
The few that came back are see-through.

164
00:10:05,844 --> 00:10:08,314
So, is it perfect? No.

165
00:10:08,949 --> 00:10:14,529
On January sixteenth, twenty twenty-six, a
power failure after overnight work

166
00:10:14,569 --> 00:10:18,259
stopped the entire Yamanote line
for about eight hours.

167
00:10:19,029 --> 00:10:22,719
Around six hundred and seventy-three
thousand people were affected.

168
00:10:23,229 --> 00:10:26,259
The government stepped in and
formally warned the railway.

169
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:29,050
And Japan is running out of workers.

170
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:34,140
The population is shrinking, and so is the number
of people who want to drive trains

171
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:35,750
at five in the morning.

172
00:10:36,340 --> 00:10:42,950
So in March twenty twenty-six, JR East raised fares for
the first time since nineteen eighty-seven.

173
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:49,150
And it plans to run the Yamanote line with no
driver at all by twenty thirty-five.

174
00:10:49,884 --> 00:10:51,974
Crowding is creeping back up too.

175
00:10:52,604 --> 00:10:57,024
After falling during the pandemic, rush-hour
trains in Tokyo have been getting

176
00:10:57,064 --> 00:10:59,034
fuller four years in a row.

177
00:10:59,487 --> 00:11:02,717
Could your city's subway survive
a Tokyo rush hour?

178
00:11:03,087 --> 00:11:04,037
Tell me in the comments.

179
00:11:04,653 --> 00:11:08,283
So that's how Tokyo moves forty
million trips a day.

180
00:11:08,793 --> 00:11:10,323
Not with perfect trains.

181
00:11:10,613 --> 00:11:14,683
With pushers and late notes,
railways that sell houses,

182
00:11:15,233 --> 00:11:17,943
crews that clean a bullet train
in seven minutes,

183
00:11:18,313 --> 00:11:20,783
and machines that can hear an
earthquake coming.

184
00:11:21,396 --> 00:11:24,666
And with millions of people who all
agree to the same rules.

185
00:11:25,116 --> 00:11:26,246
Line up at the marks.

186
00:11:26,616 --> 00:11:27,886
Let people off first.

187
00:11:28,176 --> 00:11:29,346
Don't hold the doors.

188
00:11:29,816 --> 00:11:33,846
That's why one company felt it had to
apologize for twenty seconds.

189
00:11:34,316 --> 00:11:37,506
In Tokyo, every second
belongs to everyone.

190
00:11:38,127 --> 00:11:42,137
And if you want to see how seven and a half
million people fit into Hong Kong,

191
00:11:42,467 --> 00:11:43,977
watch that story next.

192
00:11:44,507 --> 00:11:48,217
Subscribe, so you don't miss how the
next place on Earth is run.
